{
 "result": "sortints.py is built and verified, but it is not the fastest version I wrote: a faster candidate (cand.py) exists in the folder and was never adopted. The adopted sortints.py reads stdin as bytes, converts tokens to int64 with numpy, argsorts, and writes the original tokens back in sorted order, with plain-Python fallbacks for non-canonical tokens and for values beyond int64. On the 1,000,000-int file from gen.py (seed 12345, range \u00b110^9) its output is byte-identical to an independent `sorted(map(int, ...))` reference (ref.txt) and to expected.txt. Empty input prints nothing, and a small hand case sorted correctly. The registered assay (`python3 assay.py`, 1/best-of-9 wall time, 0 on any output mismatch) has a baseline of 2.2017, about 0.45 s per run, with 10.3% noise; individual runs ranged from 1.74 to 2.20. cand.py (numpy text parse, numpy sort, vectorised digit formatting) ran in about 0.19 s against 0.46 s by hand timing and matched the reference on 3000+ fuzz cases. It was not adopted because the variants round scored every version identically (179) and kept the incumbent; I did not find out why that round could not tell them apart. The one time I ran assay.py on cand.py copied in as sortints.py it scored 2.16, about the same as the incumbent, so the 0.19 s hand timing was not reproduced under the assay. The delivered body is therefore the roughly 0.45 s version, and the possible 2x gain from cand.py is unconfirmed by the assay.",
 "calls": 16,
 "secs": 952.8
